WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.240
I think the MIT Museum is a unique place, at least&nbsp;
in my experience. MIT itself of course is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:35.040
special research institute not like a lot of other&nbsp;
universities, and the museum is the only real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.600
institution that MIT has to represent the whole of&nbsp;
what it does to the rest of the world. All museums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:49.520
are cultural centers, we have the culture of MIT.&nbsp;
What makes it special, what makes it interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.280
There are no written records for most of the&nbsp;
things that we have, just the thing itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.960
So we're part archeologist, part anthropologist,&nbsp;
part historian, and we hope to imbue those same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.200
qualities in our visitors. Every museum of&nbsp;
science and technology in some sense should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.240
be a museum of ideas. Perhaps what makes this&nbsp;
one special is that many of the ideas we deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.440
with are ideas in the making, ideas that we're&nbsp;
not sure about, and some of the uncertainties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:25.840
some of the challenges of the research process&nbsp;
are also present here, and so they should be.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:35.520
The MIT Museum is home to an extraordinary&nbsp;
set of objects that define the 19th, 20th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.560
and 21st century. Doc Edgerton's negatives&nbsp;
and artifacts are among the most precious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.760
collections. They document a technology,&nbsp;
high-speed stroboscopic photography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:56.720
but they also document the desire&nbsp;
to stop time, to see what is unseen.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.120
The MIT Museum also has the world's finest&nbsp;
collection of holograms, many of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.520
on display here in the museum but much of the&nbsp;
collection is also accessible on our website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.000
Another collection that we have is probably&nbsp;
the world's best collection of robotics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.520
were created by university-based research. Robots&nbsp;
like Cogg or Kismet the humanoid social robots.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.800
Very good Kismet!

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.840
There is no other place where&nbsp;
you can see two, three dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.840
objects that tell the history of artificial&nbsp;
intelligence as well as our collection does.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:47.200
The Hart Nautical collection is a unique&nbsp;
archive of 350,000 items. The interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:54.800
thing that is perhaps unique in all nautical&nbsp;
technology collections is that we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.960
amazing amount of historic data that goes back&nbsp;
to the mid 19th century, and our collections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.600
continue right up to the present. We are actively&nbsp;
collecting technology being developed today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:14.560
for example the side scan sonar systems that we&nbsp;
have behind me. This is a type of technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:22.480
is equivalent to the development of the x-ray in&nbsp;
medical imaging. This allows us to see details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.000
on the ocean floor that were never before&nbsp;
capable. One of the things that's most vital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.600
about engaging people with interest in ocean&nbsp;
science is the giant frontier that it represents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.400
I mean, we know more about the dark side&nbsp;
of the moon than we know about the ocean.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.800
The MIT Museum is also home to one of the most&nbsp;
important architectural study collections in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.360
country.This documents the first hundred years&nbsp;
of architectural instruction at MIT which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.840
the first academic program in architecture in&nbsp;
the country. The recent exhibition Front + Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:02.240
in our Compton Gallery featured our earliest&nbsp;
architectural drawing, attributed to a renaissance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.120
architect named Baldassare Peruzzi, dating from&nbsp;
the 16th century. We pulled it apart, looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.320
all the layers of the drawing, subjected it to&nbsp;
scientific analysis, we also took the drawing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:19.200
the design labs to 3D model the drawing&nbsp;
to look at contemporary approaches to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.080
a renaissance drawing. In other words, we&nbsp;
had really basically no assumptions when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.160
went into the project and we came out with&nbsp;
a lot more questions than we started with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.440
but for us that was the&nbsp;
process that was interesting.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.280
Soapbox is a new program for adults in the MIT&nbsp;
Museum. The aim is to engage the wider community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.720
a little more closely in the debate that needs to&nbsp;
go on in the 21st century about how we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.760
live and live better with the fruits of science&nbsp;
and technology. The issue of global warming has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.440
risen to the top of the public's agenda of&nbsp;
environmental concerns. We've done everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.760
from biomedical issues to ways in which new&nbsp;
nanotechnologies, new materials, will be changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.720
everyday life. After the speakers have presented&nbsp;
the issues for the evening, the audience breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.040
up into small discussion groups and brainstorms&nbsp;
what they've heard and it's on the basis of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.720
the discussion groups come back with that&nbsp;
we organize the rest of the conversation.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.920
So what we're trying to say is, here's a place&nbsp;
where you can take part in the conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.040
that in the end will help shape the way&nbsp;
we and our children live in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.240
We'd like people to come to think of the MIT&nbsp;
Museum as an innovative place itself, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.760
the innovations that we are leading at the moment&nbsp;
is to create a new Cambridge Science Festival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.840
The idea is to have nine or ten&nbsp;
days each year in the spring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.840
when we open up the entire world of&nbsp;
science and technology in this city.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.440
What we're planning is a whole series of events&nbsp;
scattered across the community. We're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:08.080
about plays, science writing, events, exhibits,&nbsp;
a careers day for young people, and the hope is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.640
that anybody who's awake in Cambridge for that 10&nbsp;
days will realize that the science festival is on.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.800
The question is not so much when will&nbsp;
we build a robot that is a human being…

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:26.160
We have this extraordinary situation where we&nbsp;
can work with colleagues, with grad students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.280
and others to try to open up what they're doing&nbsp;
for the wider community, and it's that notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.320
that really is at the heart of what we're trying&nbsp;
to do with the MIT Museum as we look forward. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:42.160
means that we need a lot more space, and it means&nbsp;
we also want to expand the range of what we offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.600
into subject areas where the museum&nbsp;
historically hasn't done very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.840
We are in the middle of an exciting renovation&nbsp;
of a ground floor gallery space that will open up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:59.040
our collections and our programs and our&nbsp;
exhibitions quite literally to an outside audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.880
on Mass Ave. For the first time, we now have&nbsp;
ground level visibility for the museum. It's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.560
important step for us and that space will allow&nbsp;
us to have new exhibitions and new dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.240
public program spaces which will mean that we&nbsp;
can do things like Soapbox much more easily.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.800
When uncoiled, the DNA in one cell&nbsp;
of your body is six feet long…

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.960
Science is something for everyone. Technology and&nbsp;
science matter. That's the most important message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.240
that I hope people leave this museum with.&nbsp;
We do see ourselves as having that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:38.800
inclusivity, and that accessibility agenda.&nbsp;
An agenda to try to draw people in to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:57.840
benefit from, to enjoy, and if they want to&nbsp;
challenge what goes on in a place like MIT.

